From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: keep the CPU PLL VCO inside the V1.00 window

What. Raise A733_PLL_VCO_MIN from 480 to 650 MHz. ccu_a733_cpu_pll_find()
already searches N only inside the VCO window and EXT_DIVP over /1, /2,
/4, so the constant is the whole code change; the comments that quoted
the old floor follow it. At the 26 MHz DCXO the legal N range becomes
25..86 (was 19..86), and two operating points change encoding:

  OPP      before                        after
  494 MHz  N=19, EXT_DIVP /1, VCO 494    N=38, EXT_DIVP /2, VCO 988
  247 MHz  N=19, EXT_DIVP /2, VCO 494    N=38, EXT_DIVP /4, VCO 988

Every other rate keeps its (N, EXT_DIVP): 416 MHz = N=32 /2 (VCO 832),
780..2002 MHz = N=30..77 /1. No OPP is added or dropped, no voltage
changes. The lowest reachable rate moves from 123.5 to 162.5 MHz
(N=25 /4), and the probe line reads "N 25..86".

Why. UM V1.00 raised the CPU PLL VCO floor on purpose:

  * Revision log, rev 0.93 (V1.00 p. ii / PDF p. 3): the Fvco entries for
    the three CPU PLLs in Table 4-2 were updated, and the
    descriptions of the PLL_EN, PLL_PLL_EN and PLL_N bits
    in section 5.1.10 were revised.
  * Table 4-2 (V1.00 p. 20 / PDF p. 41): Fvco "650M~2.26G" for all
    three. V0.92 had "480M~2.26G" (p. 20 / PDF p. 40).
  * 5.1.10.6 CPU_L_PLL_CTRL_REG (V1.00 p. 288-289 / PDF p. 309-310),
    bit 31 gives the VCO input range as
    650 MHz to 2.26 GHz; the PLL_N note bounds InputFreq*N/P/(M0*M1) to 650..2260 MHz.
    The same for CPU_B (5.1.10.18, p. 296-297 / PDF p. 317-318) and
    CPU_DSU (5.1.10.30, p. 304-305 / PDF p. 325-326). V0.92 5.1.10.6
    had 480 in both places (p. 288-289 / PDF p. 308-309).

The 494 and 247 MHz points of arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply
ran the VCO at 494 MHz, 156 MHz under that floor. Both points are ours,
below the vendor's lowest; every vendor point in `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu`'s register dataset
(416..2002 MHz) already has its VCO at 780 MHz or above.

Why EXT_DIVP and not M0/M1. Table 4-2 lists M0 (1~4) and M1 (1~16) as
the CPU PLLs' post-dividers, but the PLL_N note bounds
InputFreq*N/P/(M0*M1) -- the output after M0*M1 -- by the same
650..2260 MHz, so M0/M1 cannot reach below 650 MHz under that reading.
EXT_DIVP (CPU_x_PLL_CLK_REG[17:16], 00 /1, 01 /2, 10 /4, 11 reserved;
5.1.10.13 p. 294 / PDF p. 315, 5.1.10.25 p. 302 / PDF p. 323, 5.1.10.37
p. 310 / PDF p. 331) sits after the PLL and under neither statement; with
M0 = M1 = 1, as `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu` keeps them, the new encodings satisfy both. Its
note (below 288 MHz output, the factor P may divide
down to the wanted rate) names a use, not a
limit: the vendor stack runs its 416 MHz point as N=32 /2 (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu`'s
dataset, both boards), and so has this driver since 2026-07-22.

One caveat stays true and does not matter here. V1.00
contradicts its own floor: the CTRL_REG reset value 0x4080_1400 has
N=20, and a bit 31 note puts the CPU_L_PLL output at
480 MHz with a 24 MHz DCXO -- 520 MHz at the 26 MHz DCXO, below 650 either way.
CPU_L's CTRL_STAT note (5.1.10.17, p. 295 /
PDF p. 316) says "650MHz to 3GHz": the same floor, a different ceiling
that nothing here approaches. Which window the silicon really has, the
UM does not settle, and this patch does not need it settled: every VCO
the driver can now program lies in 650..2236 MHz, inside the V0.92 and
the V1.00 window alike.

Verified (local, 2026-09-25; not hardware-proven):
ccu_a733_cpu_pll_find(), copied unchanged out of the patched file into a
small host-side test program, maps all fourteen OPP rates of both clusters to the
encodings above, each exact, and 416/780..2002 MHz to the same
(N, EXT_DIVP) as before the change. The series applies at fuzz 0 on
6.18.53 (229/229); drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-cpu.o builds with
W=1 without a warning, and its object code holds 649999999 (650 MHz - 1,
from DIV_ROUND_UP) twice and 479999999 nowhere; checkpatch --strict finds
nothing in the code. Nothing has run on silicon.

Pending on silicon. Per OPP, cluster pinned with the userspace governor,
read at the register, not sysfs:
  * 494 MHz: CPU_x_PLL_CTRL_REG and CTRL_STAT (+0x2c) bits[15:8] = 0x26,
    LOCK (bit 28) set, PLL_UPDATE (bit 26) clear; CPU_x_PLL_CLK_REG
    (+0x1c) bits[17:16] = 0b01, bits[26:24] = 0b011.
  * 247 MHz: the same N, CLK_REG bits[17:16] = 0b10. No record in this
    tree shows EXT_DIVP /4 on silicon -- the vendor's lowest point uses
    /2 -- so 247 MHz also needs the core rate measured (a cycle loop
    against CLOCK_MONOTONIC on a core of that cluster), not only read.
  * every other OPP: the (N, EXT_DIVP) of the 2026-07-22 record.
  * probe: "A733 CPU PLLs on a 26000000 Hz reference, N 25..86".
Falsifier: any other encoding, LOCK clear, or a measured 247 MHz point
that is not a quarter of 988 MHz.

Not changed here: the comments in arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply
still give 494 MHz as N=19 /1 "inside the 480-2260 MHz window" and
247 MHz as N=19 /2. Its rates and voltages stay right; those comments now
describe the old encoding.

Vendor comparison: not a port; the vendor's ladder never needed a VCO
below 780 MHz, so there is nothing to take or reject.

For a comparison run without it:
A7S_SKIP_PATCHES=clk-sunxi-ng-a733-keep-the-cpu-pll-vco-inside-the-v1.00-window

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-cpu.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-cpu.c
@@ -94,8 +94,14 @@
 /*
  * CPU_L/B/DSU_PLL_CTRL_REG, UM 5.1.10.6.
  *   f   = InputFreq * N / P / (M0 * M1)
- *   VCO = InputFreq * N / P,  480 MHz <= VCO <= 2260 MHz
+ *   VCO = InputFreq * N / P,  650 MHz <= VCO <= 2260 MHz
  * PLL_N is used raw (N = PLL_N, no +1); P, M0 and M1 are field+1.
+ *
+ * The 650 MHz floor is UM V1.00's, for all three PLLs (5.1.10.6, 5.1.10.18,
+ * 5.1.10.30 and Table 4-2; revision 0.93 changed it on purpose). V0.92 said
+ * 480 MHz. V1.00 contradicts itself once - its reset default N = 20 is a
+ * 520 MHz VCO at the 26 MHz DCXO - but no rate programmed here leaves the
+ * narrower window, so it is inside both and the stricter one costs nothing.
  */
 #define A733_PLL_EN			BIT(31)
 #define A733_PLL_LDO_EN			BIT(30)
@@ -108,9 +114,9 @@
 #define A733_PLL_N			GENMASK(15, 8)
 #define A733_PLL_M1			GENMASK(3, 0)
 
-#define A733_PLL_VCO_MIN		480000000UL
+#define A733_PLL_VCO_MIN		650000000UL
 #define A733_PLL_VCO_MAX		2260000000UL
-/* "PLL N is 4/5/8/9/10/12-255" - the 480 MHz floor keeps us in the dense part. */
+/* "PLL N is 4/5/8/9/10/12-255" - the VCO floor keeps us in the dense part. */
 #define A733_PLL_N_MIN			12
 #define A733_PLL_N_MAX			255
 
@@ -138,14 +144,16 @@
  *            never sees the raw PLL output, it sees the divided one.
  *
  * The driver keeps PLL_P (and M0, M1) at 1 and varies only N and EXT_DIVP.
- * Reason: with a 26 MHz reference, N alone covers 494..2236 MHz in 26 MHz steps
+ * Reason: with a 26 MHz reference, N alone covers 650..2236 MHz in 26 MHz steps
  * and every additional degree of freedom only creates second encodings of rates
  * N can already express - which would make determine_rate() non-deterministic
  * without buying a single new frequency. Keeping M0 = M1 = 1 has a second
- * benefit: the UM states the 480..2260 MHz window twice, once against the VCO
+ * benefit: the UM states the 650..2260 MHz window twice, once against the VCO
  * (bit31 note) and once against the output (PLL_N note), and those two
  * statements only coincide when M0 * M1 == 1. With M0 = M1 = 1 there is no
- * ambiguity left to get wrong.
+ * ambiguity left to get wrong. It also decides how a rate below the floor is
+ * reached: through EXT_DIVP, which neither statement covers, never through
+ * M0/M1, whose output the PLL_N note holds to the same 650 MHz floor.
  *
  * The vendor driver models "p" as _SUNXI_CCU_DIV(16, 2) with a private p_reg
  * pointing at 0x101c - that is EXT_DIVP, and PLL_P is not modelled by it at all.
@@ -198,10 +206,15 @@
  * Pick the (N, EXT_DIVP) pair giving the highest rate <= @rate. N is searched
  * only inside the VCO window, so an illegal setting cannot be selected: 416 MHz
  * for instance is unreachable with EXT_DIVP = /1 (26 * 16 = 416 MHz would put
- * the VCO 64 MHz below the floor) and is found as N = 32 with EXT_DIVP = /2 -
+ * the VCO 234 MHz below the floor) and is found as N = 32 with EXT_DIVP = /2 -
  * exactly what boot0 and the vendor stack program on both reference boards.
- * EXT_DIVP is tried in ascending order and ties keep the earlier hit, so /1 wins
- * whenever a rate is reachable both ways (780 MHz = 30x/1 and = 60x/2).
+ * 494 and 247 MHz come out the same way: N = 38 with /2 and /4, VCO 988 MHz.
+ * The UM's EXT_DIVP note (outputs below 288 MHz may use the P divider)
+ * names a use, not a limit: boot0's own 416 MHz
+ * runs on /2. EXT_DIVP is tried in ascending order and ties keep the earlier
+ * hit, so the smaller divider - the lower VCO - wins whenever a rate is
+ * reachable both ways (780 MHz = 30x/1 and = 60x/2, 494 MHz = 38x/2 and
+ * = 76x/4).
  */
 static unsigned long ccu_a733_cpu_pll_find(unsigned long parent,
 					   unsigned long rate,
@@ -672,9 +685,9 @@
 
 	/*
 	 * Publish the reachable window, derived from the actual reference rather
-	 * than hardcoded: N is bounded by the 480..2260 MHz VCO limits, the top
+	 * than hardcoded: N is bounded by the 650..2260 MHz VCO limits, the top
 	 * end is N_max with EXT_DIVP = /1 and the bottom end N_min with /4. At
-	 * 26 MHz that is 123.5 MHz .. 2236 MHz.
+	 * 26 MHz that is N 25..86, 162.5 MHz .. 2236 MHz.
 	 */
 	n_min = max_t(unsigned int, A733_PLL_N_MIN,
 		      DIV_ROUND_UP(A733_PLL_VCO_MIN, dcxo_rate));
